WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.510 --> 00:00:05.609
A year has passed since Andriy left the frontline
for home.

00:00:05.609 --> 00:00:09.540
The fighting may now be hundreds of kilometers
away, but the conflict still hasn’t let

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:11.470
him go.

00:00:11.470 --> 00:00:15.790
Time and time again, his thoughts turn to
the Donbas.

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:21.910
"I was dragging a wounded friend through the
snow to safety.

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:25.289
You fall down, and the bullets are flying
over your head.

00:00:25.289 --> 00:00:29.090
You get a few metres further, and just end
up falling down again -- because the fire

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:30.090
is so heavy."

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:34.800
Andriy spent three years on the front lines
in Donbas.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:38.359
As tensions rose, he volunteered with the
nationalist Azov Battalion.

00:00:38.359 --> 00:00:42.199
It was simply the fastest way to get to the
front, he tells us.

00:00:42.199 --> 00:00:44.999
Later, he served as a paramedic.

00:00:44.999 --> 00:00:48.850
The constant vigilance that saved Andriy’s
life in the trenches is now getting in the

00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:50.929
way of adjusting to normal life.

00:00:50.929 --> 00:00:55.780
He's just one of thousands veterans returning
home to find that the war continues in their

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:56.859
minds and their bodies.

00:00:56.859 --> 00:00:58.410
Andriy’s system releases adrenaline apparently
at random.

00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:02.179
"It happens really suddenly – everything
can be fine and then (clicks his fingers)

00:01:02.179 --> 00:01:05.870
I get all hot, I lose control over my arms
and legs.

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:09.400
My heart starts beating like crazy and my
blood pressure jumps.

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:12.760
Sometimes I get a full-scale panic attack."

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:18.570
Those are Just some of the symptoms of post-traumatic
stress disorder, PTSD.

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:22.720
But for all that, Andriy sometimes wishes
he could return to the front line.

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:25.480
Life as a civilian isn’t easy.

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:31.310
He doesn’t expect gratitude, Andriy tells
us, but even finding work is a struggle.

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:37.060
Many businesses won’t employ veterans -- for
fear they could turn violent.

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:42.750
Andriy has yet to receive any support from
the government.

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:45.130
We put this to the Ukrainian military.

00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:51.420
They deny they have a major problem on their
hands.

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:55.000
"Of course, soldiers returning from battle
have trouble re-adjusting.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:02.710
That could be anything from heightened aggressivity
to anxiety or depression.

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:14.910
But that all passes after three days at home."

00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:19.760
More than 600 soldiers on active duty have
taken their own lives since the conflict began.

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:21.870
So says Ukraine’s top military prosecutor.

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:26.230
It’s a figure that’s been making headlines
in Ukraine for months.

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:33.260
But the army top brass dismisses the statistic.

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:39.109
"The true numbers are no higher than the Ukrainian
average.

00:02:39.109 --> 00:02:47.219
It would be wrong to say that we have a critical
situation with suicides in the Ukrainian army."

00:02:47.219 --> 00:02:52.370
Instead, activists are filling the gap left
by government.

00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:56.790
We’re in Verkhovina, high up in the Carpathian
Mountains, about as far away as you can get

00:02:56.790 --> 00:02:59.079
from the front line.

00:02:59.079 --> 00:03:02.739
Veterans like Andriy can come here to take
a break and receive support from psychologists

00:03:02.739 --> 00:03:04.540
and art therapists.

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:08.200
It's an opportunity that’s only possible
thanks to private donations.

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:14.099
For the next ten days, Andriy will be sharing
every moment with the group, none of whom

00:03:14.099 --> 00:03:15.900
he knew before coming here.

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:20.140
"I don’t want to see any bunnies, any flowers!

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:26.510
All I want you to do is experiment with the
colours."

00:03:26.510 --> 00:03:29.680
Before the war Vitalina Maslova was an art
teacher.

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:34.420
What began as volunteering soon became a full-time
job, helping veterans to find their feet.

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:37.669
But how do you get adults, let alone soldiers,
to paint?

00:03:37.669 --> 00:03:41.969
"I always say: just do it, try.

00:03:41.969 --> 00:03:46.599
Our participants are always astonished when
they see that they can produce something of

00:03:46.599 --> 00:03:48.540
beauty.

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:52.879
What we’re doing here is to create an atmosphere
of trust, in a safe place.

00:03:52.879 --> 00:03:56.700
We’re surrounded by a beautiful landscape.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:00.439
They can finally let go, and think about what
they want to do with their lives.

00:04:00.439 --> 00:04:03.349
"It’s been a while!

00:04:03.349 --> 00:04:13.090
(Vitalina interrupts him – come on, it’s
been 35 years at least – laughs) I’ve

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:14.809
never done anything like this.

00:04:14.809 --> 00:04:19.010
I’m glad I came here, I just in a really
good mood."

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:24.220
The group heads up the mountain, towards a
cabin that Vitalina hopes will eventually

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:26.620
become a permanent home for the project.

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:28.660
But it's not just about the landscape.

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:33.940
"The villagers help our veterans to make sense
of who it was they were actually fighting

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:34.940
for.

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:36.930
They meet people who truly are grateful to
them.

00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:41.090
That’s really important."

00:04:41.090 --> 00:04:46.310
Chopping wood, picking apples or just letting
go: it’s a welcome break after an intense

00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:48.220
few days.

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:52.730
No-one can be sure how lasting an impact such
a short stay can have, but already Andriy

00:04:52.730 --> 00:04:54.620
seems like a changed man.

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:58.060
One with a plan.

00:04:58.060 --> 00:05:04.600
"I want to use the experiences I’ve gained
here to help others like me.

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:09.820
I’ve seen myself how tough it is.

00:05:09.820 --> 00:05:18.560
I left the army a year ago, and still can’t
find peace."

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:20.060
It won’t be easy.

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:24.450
The waiting list for this project is already
closed, Vitalina tells us.

00:05:24.450 --> 00:05:26.280
The donations have simply dried up.

